 <h2 class="text-lg font-black uppercase text-amber-700 mb-3">Key Takeaways</h2>
 <ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>Match power bank capacity to your target retail price point, not just mAh</strong>. 5,000mAh retails $12-19; 10,000mAh retails $19-29; 20,000mAh retails $29-49; 20,000mAh 65W laptop-capable retails $49-79. The highest-margin segment is 10,000mAh with digital display and built-in cables ($25-35 retail, $5-7 FOB at MOQ 500).</li>
 <li><strong>3 non-negotiable specs for 2026 OEM power banks</strong>: (1) USB-C PD 3.1 with PPS (covers iPhone, Samsung, Pixel, and laptops with one port), (2) at least one USB-A port for legacy device compatibility, (3) pass-through charging (charge the power bank while it charges your device). Missing any of these = lost sales to competitors who include them.</li>
 <li><strong>Cell choice determines your warranty liability</strong>: Grade-A cells (LG, Samsung SDI, Panasonic) deliver rated cycles with under 2% failure rate over 12 months. Grade-B cells (generic Chinese) save $0.30-0.50/unit but generate 5-8% warranty returns. For a 10,000-unit order, that's the difference between 200 returns and 800 returns, the cell savings are wiped out by return shipping and replacement costs.</li>
 <li><strong>Built-in cable vs port-only: built-in cables increase retail conversion by 18-25%</strong> (consumers prefer grab-and-go) but add $0.50-1.00 to BOM cost and are the #1 mechanical failure point. The 2026 sweet spot: one built-in USB-C cable + one additional USB-C port + one USB-A port. Cover the convenience buyer and the power user in one SKU.</li>
 </ul>
